# Manuscript PDF page 223

                              THE CHORD



   I couldn’t answer. I was in a courtyard in the mountains with thread
across my fingers.
   Lucía Huamán’s hands came first, then the lamp. Her sister Ana
held it out of the wind with her whole body. The air was thin enough
that I felt it in my chest. Behind the courtyard wall, older stone kept the
dark.
   Lucía had warned Mama Tete that she would not stage a ceremony
she didn’t keep. She kept her family’s way. Her fingers worked a figure
into the thread, undid it, reopened it, and the tone of that place came
through in the smallest choices: a click, a hesitation, a quarter turn of
the wrist. When Ana said something, she stopped.
   Listen to the person beside you. Then go on.
   It was the simplest instruction I’d ever been given, and I’d been fail‐
ing it all spring.
   Manny’s mouth was next to my ear. I could see the shape of my
name on his lips. On my right nothing came through.
   I swung my head toward him and the hall heeled over like a boat in
a swell.
   Yanaisa snatched the stone out of my wrist’s path, moved Yúcahu a
palm’s width closer to the coin, and the load on my arm split in two.
   “Let me carry my half,” she said.
   I’d been carrying her half in my own fist.
   I opened two fingers.
   Lucía’s thread went on without me.



Tomás Aguilar sat on a stool at the edge of his yard with a mason’s
hammer across his thighs. His son Emiliano ran a spirit level along a
low garden wall that didn’t need leveling; it was a way to keep his
hands busy. The wall was fitted volcanic stone with no mortar, the kind
of work that looks like luck until you try to push it over. Tomás had
built it himself, and it was far too beautiful for a garden wall.
   He turned the hammer in his hand and struck the wall with the flat
of the head.




